# `table.set(base.name, base); table.set(base.name, base);` in a turn of a loop. Each statement had a test of its own that the Map is as made, both arms walked [the read of the method .. its call] and met; and
# `base.name` was a read that asks for itself whether the shape fits and the slot is filled: an empty slot goes up the prototypes and may run code. So at the second statement nothing was known, and the Map's set was
# called twice. TypeScript 6 has the two statements in addInheritedMembers(): 0.86 million turns, about 60 M instructions.
# In a loop, calls of methods of one Map or Set that stand in a row in one block are walked behind ONE test, where nothing in the row runs code on the typed arm: between a read of a method and its call stand only
# conversions and reads by name off holders from in front of which one layout is expected. In front of the row: the holder's shape; is the slot filled; who fails goes the closed arm, where the row stands as
# written. The slot is read once. A set, or an add, straight behind the same one (the same table, key and value) is dropped: it finds the key and stores what is there.
# -- source: main.ts
